%U A401888 64367752896358854888679321545,26734418610631407725439418180202961,18204991153590543102807457619597653886481,67996208124059517569572096053649191187215989529
%N A401888 Number of n X n matrices M over GF(3) satisfying M^2 = 0.
%C A401888 The zero matrix is included.
%C A401888 If M^2 = 0 and rank(M) = r, then im(M) is contained in ker(M), so 0 <= r <= floor(n/2).
%C A401888 More generally, over a finite field GF(q), where q is a prime power, the number of square-zero matrices of rank r is [n,r]_q * product{j=0..r-1}(q^(n-r)-q^j), where [n,r]_q denotes a Gaussian binomial coefficient. This sequence corresponds to q = 3.
%C A401888 To count matrices of rank r, choose U = im(M), an r-dimensional subspace of V, then choose K = ker(M), an (n-r)-dimensional subspace containing U, and finally choose an isomorphism V/K -> U. The numbers of choices are [n,r]_q, [n-r,r]_q and |GL(r,q)|, respectively.
%C A401888 Every square-zero matrix of rank r has Jordan form with r blocks of size 2 and n-2r blocks of size 1. Thus there is exactly one similarity class for each possible rank.
%C A401888 A053764 counts all nilpotent n X n matrices over GF(3), not just those satisfying M^2 = 0. Since every square-zero matrix is nilpotent, a(n) <= 3^(n^2-n). Equality holds for n = 0, 1, 2, and the inequality is strict for n >= 3. The sequences first differ at n = 3: 105 versus 729.
%C A401888 A053722 is the analogous square-zero matrix count over GF(2). In characteristic 2, M^2 = I if and only if (I+M)^2 = 0.
%C A401888 The rank-wise formula used here is a special case of a general counting formula for square-zero matrices over finite fields.
%D A401888 D. Dolžan, The Wiener Index and the Wiener Complexity of the Zero-Divisor Graph of a Ring, Bull. Iran. Math. Soc. 51 (2025), Article 10.
%F A401888 a(n) = Sum_{r=0..floor(n/2)} [n,r]_3 * Product_{j=0..r-1} (3^(n-r)-3^j).
%F A401888 [n,r]_q = Product_{j=0..r-1} (q^n-q^j) / (q^r-q^j).
%e A401888 a(2) = 9, consisting of one rank-zero matrix and eight rank-one matrices.
%e A401888 a(4) = 7281, with rank-wise contributions 1, 1040 and 6240.
%e A401888 a(6) = 449336889, with rank-wise contributions 1, 88088, 68708640 and 380540160.
%o A401888 (Python)
%o A401888 from math import prod
%o A401888 def gaussian_binomial(n, k, q=3):
%o A401888     numerator = prod(q**n - q**j for j in range(k))
%o A401888     denominator = prod(q**k - q**j for j in range(k))
%o A401888     return numerator // denominator
%o A401888 def a(n, q=3):
%o A401888     return sum(
%o A401888         gaussian_binomial(n, r, q)
%o A401888         * prod(q**(n-r) - q**j for j in range(r))
%o A401888         for r in range(n//2 + 1)
%o A401888     )
%o A401888 print([a(n) for n in range(15)])
%Y A401888 Cf. A053722, A053764.
%K A401888 nonn,new
